{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2398 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "723687702ef6fe9159b860d1ff56e1fbc254d3bed52095be448154e027b2876f" :edb/db-before-t 2397 :edb/db-after-t 2398 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535710 50 #inst "2026-10-09T05:43:30.536Z" 13194139535710 true] [17592186105583 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of students that passed the exam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=5$ students\n\n- the probability that each student passed the exam is $p=75\\%=0.75$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(5,0.75).$ We want to find the probability that $x=3$ of the students passed the exam.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})(0.75)^{3}(1-0.75)^{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!}(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =10(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =0.264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186105583 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of students that passed the exam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=5$ students\n- the probability that each student passed the exam is $p=75\\%=0.75$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(5,0.75)$. We want to find the probability that $x=3$ of the students passed the exam.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 3) &= (\\frac{5}{3})(0.75)^{3}(1 - 0.75)^{5 - 3} \\\\\n&= (5!)/(3!(5 - 3)!)(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\\n&= 10(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.264\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 false] [17592186105584 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ rolls\n\n- the probability of rolling an even number on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{3}{6} = 0.5\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(8,0.5).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(1-0.5)^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of rolling an even number at least $7$ times. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 7) & =P(X=7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8})(0.5)^{8}(0.5)^{8-8} \\\\  & =8(0.5)^{7}(0.5)+1(0.5)^{8} \\\\  & =0.035\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186105584 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ rolls\n- the probability of rolling an even number on any roll of the die is\n$p=\\dfrac{3}{6} = 0.5$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(8,0.5)$.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = x) &= (\\frac{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(1 - 0.5)^{8 - x} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{8 - x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of rolling an even number at least $7$ times. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 7) &= P(X = 7) + P(X = 8) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{8}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{8 - 7} + (\\frac{8}{8})(0.5)^{8}(0.5)^{8 - 8} \\\\\n&= 8(0.5)^{7}(0.5) + 1(0.5)^{8} \\\\\n&= 0.035\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 false] [17592186105585 1100 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The ages of $20$ college professors\n\n- The number of questions answered correctly by a student in a $12$-question exam\n\n- The heights of $10$ elementary school children" 13194139535710 true] [17592186105585 1100 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n1. The ages of $20$ college professors\n2. The number of questions answered correctly by a student in a $12$-question exam\n3. The heights of $10$ elementary school children" 13194139535710 false] [17592186105585 1145 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of questions answered in a $12$- question exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $12$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The heights cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535710 true] [17592186105585 1145 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of questions answered in a $12$- question exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $12$ independent experiments.\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The heights cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535710 false] [17592186105588 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the player wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=40\\%=0.4$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(12,0.4).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{12-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{12-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the player wins at least 2 of the games. This corresponds to $P(X \\geq 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2) = P(X=2) + P(X=3) + \\ldots + P(X=12)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{12-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{12-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.6)^{12}+12(0.4)(0.6)^{11}] \\\\  & =1-0.019\\,591 \\\\  & =0.980\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186105588 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the player wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ games\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=40\\%=0.4$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(12,0.4)$.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = x) &= (\\frac{12}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1 - 0.4)^{12 - x} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{12}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{12 - x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the player wins at least 2 of the games. This corresponds to\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2)\n$$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2) = P(X=2) + P(X=3) + \\ldots + P(X=12)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 2) &= 1 - P(X < 2) \\\\\n&= 1 - [P(X = 0) + P(X = 1)] \\\\\n&= 1 - [(\\frac{12}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{12 - 0} + (\\frac{12}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{12 - 1}] \\\\\n&= 1 - [(0.6)^{12} + 12(0.4)(0.6)^{11}] \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.019591 \\\\\n&= 0.980\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 false] [17592186105746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362572 13194139535710 true] [17592186105746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362607 13194139535710 true] [17592186105746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362610 13194139535710 true] [17592186105746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362613 13194139535710 true] [17592186105746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362666 13194139535710 true] [17592186105747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362579 13194139535710 true] [17592186105747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362586 13194139535710 true] [17592186105747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362593 13194139535710 true] [17592186105747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362617 13194139535710 true] [17592186105747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362631 13194139535710 true] [17592186105748 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362565 13194139535710 true] [17592186105748 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362624 13194139535710 true] [17592186105748 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362652 13194139535710 true] [17592186105748 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362659 13194139535710 true] [17592186105751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362600 13194139535710 true] [17592186105751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362621 13194139535710 true] [17592186105751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362638 13194139535710 true] [17592186105751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362645 13194139535710 true] [17592186106440 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535710 true] [17592186106440 1100 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The weights of $30$ professional wrestlers\n- The number of times that the number $6$ appears when a fair die is rolled $20$ times\n- The price of milk at the store" 13194139535710 true] [17592186106440 1100 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n1. The weights of $30$ professional wrestlers\n2. The number of times that the number $6$ appears when a fair die is rolled $20$ times\n3. The price of milk at the store" 13194139535710 false] [17592186106440 1145 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The weights cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of times that the number $6$ appears when a die is rolled $20$ times can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $20$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The prices cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535710 true] [17592186106445 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535710 true] [17592186106445 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA golfer has a $10%$ chance of winning each tournament they enter. If the golfer enters $20$ tournaments, what is the probability that they win at least $2$ tournaments? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 false] [17592186106445 1100 "A golfer has a $10%$ chance of winning each tournament they enter. If the golfer enters $20$ tournaments, what is the probability that they win at least $2$ tournaments? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186106445 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of tournaments that the golfer wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=10%=0.1$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,0.1).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.1)^{x}(1-0.1)^{20-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.1)^{x}(0.9)^{20-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the golfer wins at least $2$ tournaments. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=20)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{0})(0.1)^{0}(0.9)^{20-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{1})(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{20-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.9)^{20}+20(0.1)(0.9)^{19}] \\\\  & =1-0.391\\,747 \\\\  & =0.608\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362565 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362566 13194139535710 true] [17592186362565 1089 "q-130376" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362565 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535710 true] [17592186362565 1100 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of students who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected primary school children\n- The number of three-point shots made by a basketball player in 15 shots\n- The number of purchases made with a credit card in a store out of $10$ observed purchases, if, on average, $75%$ of all purchases are made with a credit card" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362565 1113 #uuid "00947d44-f147-5655-8d88-1b3aaa7c544b" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362565 1145 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of children who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected students can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $200$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of three-point shots in $15$ shots can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $15$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of purchases made with a credit card in $10$ purchases can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362567 13194139535710 true] [17592186362566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362568 13194139535710 true] [17592186362566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362569 13194139535710 true] [17592186362566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362570 13194139535710 true] [17592186362566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362571 13194139535710 true] [17592186362566 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535710 true] [17592186362566 1041 "selection" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362566 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362567 13194139535710 true] [17592186362566 1110 #uuid "5b0e1759-9ae0-5f7a-8c53-6d26ca6e0d18" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362567 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362567 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362567 1111 #uuid "db434826-72e1-5b0b-a64e-cd8f681ca588" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362568 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362568 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362568 1111 #uuid "659859e4-f7dd-5be4-ada0-a95ec768b357" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362569 1034 "III only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362569 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362569 1111 #uuid "4e5a6a19-848b-57f5-8875-9ac54708c707" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362570 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362570 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362570 1111 #uuid "61d3f12e-1b75-5e74-bd73-714069ac429c" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362571 1034 "II  only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362571 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362571 1111 #uuid "a799cce2-c14f-51a2-87e1-c6e3f278cabc" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362572 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362573 13194139535710 true] [17592186362572 1089 "q-130383" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362572 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535710 true] [17592186362572 1100 "The probability that John succeeds in selling an insurance policy to a given customer is $0.45.$ On a given day, he interacts with $8$ customers. Assuming that the transactions with each customer are independent, what is the probability that he will sell exactly $2$ insurance policies on this day? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362572 1113 #uuid "1db231fa-25cb-50bf-aeea-fb3a82c53299" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362572 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of insurance policies that John sells on this day.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ customers\n\n- the probability that John succeeds in selling an insurance policy to a given customer is $p=0.45$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,0.45).$ We want to find the probability that he will sell exactly $2$ insurance policies.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2})(0.45)^{2}(1-0.45)^{8-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8!}{2!(8-2)!}(0.45)^{2}(0.55)^{6} \\\\  & =28(0.45)^{2}(0.55)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.157\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362574 13194139535710 true] [17592186362573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362575 13194139535710 true] [17592186362573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362576 13194139535710 true] [17592186362573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362577 13194139535710 true] [17592186362573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362578 13194139535710 true] [17592186362573 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535710 true] [17592186362573 1041 "selection" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362573 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362574 13194139535710 true] [17592186362573 1110 #uuid "6049ed22-210f-5012-ba93-8d31f5a47e6c" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362574 1034 "0.157" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362574 1111 #uuid "1d1a17ae-cb25-5faa-8bfd-4d271840ce35" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362575 1034 "0.071" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362575 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362575 1111 #uuid "9163f413-bf9b-51cd-89ea-175faa3941de" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362576 1034 "0.257" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362576 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362576 1111 #uuid "ee5cbb3b-fcf5-533c-974e-29d585b3433d" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362577 1034 "0.172" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362577 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362577 1111 #uuid "17db83ee-84fa-5e14-808a-29bee9743282" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362578 1034 "0.263" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362578 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362578 1111 #uuid "82b67541-f209-59b4-8d80-7ff571ffb443" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362579 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362580 13194139535710 true] [17592186362579 1089 "q-130573" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362579 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535710 true] [17592186362579 1100 "A math exam consists of $10$ questions, with each question containing four possible answers, where only one is the correct answer. Michael takes the exam and, for each question, he randomly chooses one of the four answers. What is the probability that Michael answers at least eight questions correctly? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362579 1113 #uuid "17a6bd62-d6c1-5592-8320-691a597425f6" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362579 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of questions Michael answers correctly.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ questions on the exam\n\n- the probability of choosing a correct answer is $p=\\frac{1}{4}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,\\frac{1}{4}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})}^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that Michael answers at least $8$ questions correctly. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 8) & =P(X=8)+P(X=9)+P(X=10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{8}){(\\frac{1}{4})}^{8}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{10-8}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9}){(\\frac{1}{4})}^{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10}){(\\frac{1}{4})}^{10}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{10-10} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{8}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+10{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}+1{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{10}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.0004\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362581 13194139535710 true] [17592186362580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362582 13194139535710 true] [17592186362580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362583 13194139535710 true] [17592186362580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362584 13194139535710 true] [17592186362580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362585 13194139535710 true] [17592186362580 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535710 true] [17592186362580 1041 "selection" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362580 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362583 13194139535710 true] [17592186362580 1110 #uuid "5fd533fb-651e-5041-b53b-7a326b772a62" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362581 1034 "0.2440" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362581 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362581 1111 #uuid "7d847e63-f8e8-5ac0-a2fd-59ec3f5b53f4" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362582 1034 "0.0107" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362582 1111 #uuid "ca29e2d6-ebde-5a30-a0ee-e0f9efbb64fe" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362583 1034 "0.0004" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362583 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362583 1111 #uuid "26ce30a9-15e3-575d-80b2-603399220c2e" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362584 1034 "0.0547" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362584 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362584 1111 #uuid "f6c827cd-1b40-5747-bdab-8ceb1903f9b5" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362585 1034 "0.5256" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362585 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362585 1111 #uuid "34eab8e0-d2d5-5b0c-a39c-961f23811b95" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362586 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362587 13194139535710 true] [17592186362586 1089 "q-130744" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362586 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535710 true] [17592186362586 1100 "In a certain school, $17.5%$ of students are left-handed. In a random sample of $8$ students from this school, what is the probability that at least $6$ students are left-handed? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362586 1113 #uuid "d6929414-119d-5b75-9482-bc1d675f0f1e" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362586 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of left-handed students in the group.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ students in the group\n\n- the probability of a student being left-handed is $p=0.175$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,0.175).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x}){(0.175)}^{x}{(1-0.175)}^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x}){(0.175)}^{x}{(0.825)}^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at least $6$ students are left-handed. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & =P(X=6)+P(X=7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{6}){(0.175)}^{6}{(0.825)}^{8-6}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7}){(0.175)}^{7}{(0.825)}^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8}){(0.175)}^{8}{(0.825)}^{8-8} \\\\  & =28{(0.175)}^{6}{(0.825)}^{2}+8{(0.175)}^{7}{(0.825)}^{1}+1{(0.175)}^{8}{(0.825)}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.0006\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362588 13194139535710 true] [17592186362587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362589 13194139535710 true] [17592186362587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362590 13194139535710 true] [17592186362587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362591 13194139535710 true] [17592186362587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362592 13194139535710 true] [17592186362587 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535710 true] [17592186362587 1041 "selection" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362587 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362590 13194139535710 true] [17592186362587 1110 #uuid "464ba014-0e1d-5c8d-b7d7-9473f5c629cd" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362588 1034 "0.0327" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362588 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362588 1111 #uuid "655f0f31-84e0-56c3-a40d-487cc4dabc95" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362589 1034 "0.0088" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362589 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362589 1111 #uuid "9d323686-7afb-5570-8175-8e081c30f762" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362590 1034 "0.0006" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362590 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362590 1111 #uuid "e10f38d7-2ce0-516d-9f97-e5f0d0581759" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362591 1034 "0.0126" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362591 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362591 1111 #uuid "498c0f18-ebe6-5860-b599-0ae0ced2012f" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362592 1034 "0.1250" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362592 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362592 1111 #uuid "ac09f856-b3ae-5e9e-a94b-becd8a4fe3f1" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362593 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362594 13194139535710 true] [17592186362593 1089 "q-130746" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362593 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535710 true] [17592186362593 1100 "A box of chocolates contains cherry creams and almond little cakes in the ratio of $2:1.$ Four chocolates are selected at random, with replacement. What is the probability of getting at most $2$ cherry creams? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362593 1113 #uuid "95b91ca4-b34b-549c-bb1a-26c0529d749b" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362593 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of selected cherry creams.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=4$ selected chocolates\n\n- since cherry creams and almond little cakes are in the ratio of $2:1,$ the probability of selecting a cherry cream from the box is $p=\\frac{2}{3}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(4,\\frac{2}{3}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting at most $2$ cherry creams. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-2} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}+4{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}+6{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.407\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362595 13194139535710 true] [17592186362594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362596 13194139535710 true] [17592186362594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362597 13194139535710 true] [17592186362594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362598 13194139535710 true] [17592186362594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362599 13194139535710 true] [17592186362594 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535710 true] [17592186362594 1041 "selection" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362594 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362597 13194139535710 true] [17592186362594 1110 #uuid "9028d049-3d04-5c2d-bfc0-3eddbd8948ca" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362595 1034 "0.329" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362595 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362595 1111 #uuid "94d7f602-ff6b-5e99-b911-d93d251b7da0" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362596 1034 "0.296" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362596 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362596 1111 #uuid "711ccb47-ecb8-5aed-b150-eb5bce323c3b" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362597 1034 "0.407" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362597 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362597 1111 #uuid "45cf1966-f172-58ed-89dd-e819d92006d3" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362598 1034 "0.156" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362598 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362598 1111 #uuid "ae8d3aab-bd6b-544f-8011-ce93152632c5" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362599 1034 "0.889" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362599 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362599 1111 #uuid "7cbe8223-0576-5868-b96b-4d0d4522b650" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362600 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362601 13194139535710 true] [17592186362600 1089 "q-130878" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362600 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535710 true] [17592186362600 1100 "A basketball player has a $70%$ success rate in shooting from the free throw line during a season. The player has ten shots from the free throw line in one match. What is the probability that the player is successful with at most eight throws? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362600 1113 #uuid "4fcb80d4-6b8d-54f6-8fb8-39ba159a3c03" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362600 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of successful free throws.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ shots from the free throw line in the match\n\n- the probability that the player is successful from the free throw line is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the player is successful with at most eight throws. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 8).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 8)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=8)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 8) & =1-P(X>8) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=9)+P(X=10)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10-10}] \\\\  & =1-[10(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{1}+1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0}] \\\\  & =1-0.149\\,308 \\\\  & ≈0.851\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362602 13194139535710 true] [17592186362601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362603 13194139535710 true] [17592186362601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362604 13194139535710 true] [17592186362601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362605 13194139535710 true] [17592186362601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362606 13194139535710 true] [17592186362601 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535710 true] [17592186362601 1041 "selection" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362601 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362605 13194139535710 true] [17592186362601 1110 #uuid "63aeb33c-da83-507c-af73-b301384365f0" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362602 1034 "0.233" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362602 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362602 1111 #uuid "3dca2c2b-28bb-569f-b2cd-24713de0112d" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362603 1034 "0.382" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362603 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362603 1111 #uuid "b0db495a-ff26-5066-9556-1523fb669171" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362604 1034 "0.687" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362604 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362604 1111 #uuid "94df3645-4bfd-5634-a1a6-c349d2f52e6b" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362605 1034 "0.851" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362605 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362605 1111 #uuid "3ffd6064-d2c4-5308-a7c3-0c0449b9c101" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362606 1034 "0.791" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362606 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362606 1111 #uuid "72cce0ad-05cb-5ba1-9c23-29af52010c62" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362607 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362608 13194139535710 true] [17592186362607 1089 "q-276118" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362607 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535710 true] [17592186362607 1100 "A fair six-sided die is rolled $5$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting a $6$ exactly two times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362607 1113 #uuid "a60095c2-6da0-5e01-9dc8-1c655b3bea85" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362607 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times that a $6$ is rolled. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ rolls.\n\n- The probability of getting a $6$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,\\frac{1}{6}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=2$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}{(1-\\frac{1}{6})}^{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!}{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{3} \\\\  & =10{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.161,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362609 13194139535710 true] [17592186362608 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535710 true] [17592186362608 1041 "field-1" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362608 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362609 13194139535710 true] [17592186362608 1110 #uuid "6f223500-1966-5321-9a91-57851edf3334" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362609 1034 "0.161" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362609 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362609 1111 #uuid "e197b6ca-1439-519f-afbc-aff2c2543b83" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362610 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362611 13194139535710 true] [17592186362610 1089 "q-331462" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362610 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535710 true] [17592186362610 1100 "A biased spinner has a $60%$ chance of landing on blue. The spinner is spun $4$ times. Rounded to three decimal places, what is the probability of getting blue exactly $2$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362610 1113 #uuid "9e9e0d94-9420-56a4-b27d-db080d52e145" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362610 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the spinner landed on blue.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=4$ spins.\n\n- The probability of landing on blue is $p=60%=0.6.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(4,0.6).$ We want to find the probability of landing on blue $x=2$ times.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})(0.6)^{2}(1-0.6)^{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!}(0.6)^{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =6(0.6)^{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.346,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362611 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362612 13194139535710 true] [17592186362611 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535710 true] [17592186362611 1041 "field-1" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362611 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362612 13194139535710 true] [17592186362611 1110 #uuid "d8bd1f4e-932f-5edf-88fc-92e4298c4bf5" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362612 1034 "0.346" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362612 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362612 1111 #uuid "5bd97b00-15bf-5928-9ee6-12974585feed" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362613 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362614 13194139535710 true] [17592186362613 1089 "q-331494" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362613 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535710 true] [17592186362613 1100 "A fair eight-sided die is rolled $6$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting a $7$ or $8$ exactly two times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362613 1113 #uuid "ae728baf-c8f8-57a9-b721-7ee1b58bb5b1" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362613 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times that a $7$ or $8$ is rolled. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=6$ rolls.\n\n- The probability of getting a $7$ or $8$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,\\frac{1}{4}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=2$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2}){(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})}^{6-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{2!(6-2)!}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{4} \\\\  & =15{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.297,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362615 13194139535710 true] [17592186362614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362616 13194139535710 true] [17592186362614 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535710 true] [17592186362614 1041 "field-1" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362614 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362615 13194139535710 true] [17592186362614 1110 #uuid "1633915a-7e35-5ab8-9eb1-031b5fc043dc" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362615 1034 "0.297" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362615 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362615 1111 #uuid "0fc018de-b4c8-51b5-bf01-170c762a2ea3" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362616 1034 "0.296" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362616 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362616 1111 #uuid "415ba8b1-2d29-535e-8828-c6ed1bca43b4" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362617 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362618 13194139535710 true] [17592186362617 1089 "q-331526" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362617 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535710 true] [17592186362617 1100 "A new software program fails to run with probability $70%.$ If it is tested on $5$ computers, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that it fails on $1$ or fewer of them?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362617 1113 #uuid "ea0d449c-1cdc-5c3f-99b6-765afc4040fa" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362617 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of failed runs.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ tests.\n\n- The probability of failure is $p=70%=0.7.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.7).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that $1$ or fewer of the $5$ software program runs fail. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.7)^{0}(0.3)^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.7)^{1}(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =1(0.3)^{5}+5(0.7)(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.031,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362619 13194139535710 true] [17592186362618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362620 13194139535710 true] [17592186362618 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535710 true] [17592186362618 1041 "field-1" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362618 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362619 13194139535710 true] [17592186362618 1110 #uuid "cb7d750b-b4ba-5ae0-8dbd-079f79dfe4f6" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362619 1034 "0.031" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362619 1111 #uuid "61733493-2bbc-5b5a-add1-f3e290e7b690" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362620 1034 "0.030" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362620 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362620 1111 #uuid "7f4d364c-df5c-5cca-b246-0e6117eeb2fb" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362621 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362622 13194139535710 true] [17592186362621 1089 "q-331923" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362621 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535710 true] [17592186362621 1100 "A text message has a $20%$ chance of being flagged as spam by a filter. Suppose $10$ messages are randomly selected. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that at least $2$ are flagged as spam?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362621 1113 #uuid "74ab2e3c-1d15-5ca2-8427-1d2761d5ef2b" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362621 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of messages flagged as spam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ messages.\n\n- The probability of a message being flagged as spam is $p=20%=0.20.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.20).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.20)^{x}(1-0.20)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.20)^{x}(0.80)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of at least $2$ messages being flagged. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.20)^{0}(0.80)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.20)^{1}(0.80)^{10-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.80)^{10}+10(0.20)(0.80)^{9}] \\\\  & =1-0.375\\,809… \\\\  & ≈0.624,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362623 13194139535710 true] [17592186362622 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535710 true] [17592186362622 1041 "field-1" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362622 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362623 13194139535710 true] [17592186362622 1110 #uuid "37054962-058b-50c9-a759-c231d31ddbdc" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362623 1034 "0.624" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362623 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362623 1111 #uuid "911d3f28-c013-5abc-82de-dd20bc9fc8b7" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362624 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362625 13194139535710 true] [17592186362624 1089 "q-84750" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362624 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535710 true] [17592186362624 1100 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of members of a school band\n- The number of goals scored by a soccer player in $10$ penalties if the probability of scoring any penalty is $65%$\n- The number of victories of a chess player in $5$ matches if the probability of the player winning any individual game is $45%$" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362624 1113 #uuid "01a6534f-43b7-5f1c-a891-d39157eb162b" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362624 1145 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of school band members cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of goals in $10$ penalties can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $5$ matches can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $5$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362625 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362626 13194139535710 true] [17592186362625 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362627 13194139535710 true] [17592186362625 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362628 13194139535710 true] [17592186362625 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362629 13194139535710 true] [17592186362625 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362630 13194139535710 true] [17592186362625 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535710 true] [17592186362625 1041 "selection" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362625 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362626 13194139535710 true] [17592186362625 1110 #uuid "4adf135c-528f-5c7a-8204-130c0d095dc2" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362626 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362626 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362626 1111 #uuid "9f0cfdbc-c977-5c15-a9f0-17a02869a8bd" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362627 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362627 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362627 1111 #uuid "5e381515-b701-5eea-8003-574fc1e1cdeb" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362628 1034 "III only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362628 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362628 1111 #uuid "894603ab-54b1-5931-89d7-54624c84e336" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362629 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362629 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362629 1111 #uuid "96c2ac8d-b330-5339-8154-8909c82632d7" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362630 1034 "II only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362630 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362630 1111 #uuid "a7d6d87d-2c77-573b-b153-7cf3ed7440ce" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362631 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362632 13194139535710 true] [17592186362631 1089 "q-84787" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362631 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535710 true] [17592186362631 1100 "A fair coin is tossed $8$ times. What is the probability of getting at most $3$ heads? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362631 1113 #uuid "f1fa5c5c-1256-58a4-a2e6-09fcb984e75a" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362631 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of \"heads\" obtained when tossing a coin.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ tosses\n\n- the probability of getting a \"head\" is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting a \"success\" (i.e., a \"head\") on at most $3$ tosses. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{1}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8-2}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{3}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8-3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8}+8(\\frac{1}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7}+28{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}+56{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} \\\\  & =0.363\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362633 13194139535710 true] [17592186362632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362634 13194139535710 true] [17592186362632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362635 13194139535710 true] [17592186362632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362636 13194139535710 true] [17592186362632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362637 13194139535710 true] [17592186362632 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535710 true] [17592186362632 1041 "selection" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362632 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362634 13194139535710 true] [17592186362632 1110 #uuid "6a40f4ed-ef04-5194-89c6-dcc679b33346" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362633 1034 "0.297" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362633 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362633 1111 #uuid "02f5288d-2dd8-528d-b671-7eaafc2e22ce" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362634 1034 "0.363" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362634 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362634 1111 #uuid "b4b68ac8-5fe2-5764-8d69-a2974341184f" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362635 1034 "0.345" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362635 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362635 1111 #uuid "e5c61903-0021-5a45-86b2-d1b30801a581" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362636 1034 "0.289" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362636 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362636 1111 #uuid "16614ff7-5702-57b7-b5a8-282cb1c1c9ad" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362637 1034 "0.328" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362637 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362637 1111 #uuid "f8faa9f9-8290-55a0-8955-64262886ccd4" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362638 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362639 13194139535710 true] [17592186362638 1089 "q-84804" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362638 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535710 true] [17592186362638 1100 "A painter at an exhibition has a $35%$ chance of selling each of his works of art. What is the probability that when exhibiting $14$ works of art, the painter sells $2$ or more? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362638 1113 #uuid "71e8d013-8b88-5657-bbca-2113b716fcd5" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362638 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of works of art sold.\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=14$ works of art\n\n- the probability of selling each work is $p=35%=0.35$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(14,0.35).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{x})(0.35)^{x}(1-0.35)^{14-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{x})(0.35)^{x}(0.65)^{14-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the painter sells $2$ or more works of art. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=14)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{0})(0.35)^{0}(0.65)^{14-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{1})(0.35)^{1}(0.65)^{14-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.65)^{14}+14(0.35)(0.65)^{13}] \\\\  & =1-0.020\\,519 \\\\  & =0.979\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362640 13194139535710 true] [17592186362639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362641 13194139535710 true] [17592186362639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362642 13194139535710 true] [17592186362639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362643 13194139535710 true] [17592186362639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362644 13194139535710 true] [17592186362639 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535710 true] [17592186362639 1041 "selection" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362639 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362641 13194139535710 true] [17592186362639 1110 #uuid "69ea4569-9a35-5df7-9c79-eb9e58857090" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362640 1034 "0.916" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362640 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362640 1111 #uuid "cca28471-c981-5a78-b4a8-b2dead0117b0" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362641 1034 "0.979" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362641 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362641 1111 #uuid "ad80dfef-a9d1-5359-96a0-d8f2d2e00e42" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362642 1034 "0.020" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362642 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362642 1111 #uuid "46944fb0-9059-5144-b521-ca81f7741f8b" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362643 1034 "0.983" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362643 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362643 1111 #uuid "3d19da8d-38bf-5ec8-acc7-9c09603f546d" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362644 1034 "0.943" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362644 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362644 1111 #uuid "1f6f2061-73ec-5645-8430-3f4e679efc3d" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362645 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362646 13194139535710 true] [17592186362645 1089 "q-84805" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362645 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535710 true] [17592186362645 1100 "A party decorator knows that one out of every five balloons he blows up pops right away. If the decorator blows up $15$ balloons, what is the probability that more than $2$ balloons pop? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362645 1113 #uuid "e6406e18-da76-59f2-961a-f419b2168ca4" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362645 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of balloons that pop.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=15$ balloons\n\n- the probability that one balloon pops is $p=\\frac{1}{5}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(15,\\frac{1}{5}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{x}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})}^{15-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{x}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting at least $3$ balloons that pop. This corresponds to $P(X>2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X>2)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{0}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{0}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{1}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{1}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{2}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15-2}] \\\\  & =1-[{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15}+15(\\frac{1}{5}){(\\frac{4}{5})}^{14}+105{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{13}] \\\\  & =1-0.398\\,023 \\\\  & =0.602\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362647 13194139535710 true] [17592186362646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362648 13194139535710 true] [17592186362646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362649 13194139535710 true] [17592186362646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362650 13194139535710 true] [17592186362646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362651 13194139535710 true] [17592186362646 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535710 true] [17592186362646 1041 "selection" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362646 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362649 13194139535710 true] [17592186362646 1110 #uuid "6fbf6e6c-ce8e-583a-ad06-5d47fdbca07f" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362647 1034 "0.398" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362647 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362647 1111 #uuid "ccaaf3e0-1ff6-51ff-8e17-e0ba15e8e884" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362648 1034 "0.592" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362648 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362648 1111 #uuid "5b5841bf-c4f0-549f-8e63-0273619d81b9" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362649 1034 "0.602" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362649 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362649 1111 #uuid "c1b7d9c4-b958-52e8-b7e9-6495219ebd79" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362650 1034 "0.351" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362650 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362650 1111 #uuid "fc01552c-81de-52fd-b74b-cb2289593700" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362651 1034 "0.648" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362651 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362651 1111 #uuid "19afb6ee-db01-54c4-a459-17c3dd9a485c" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362652 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362653 13194139535710 true] [17592186362652 1089 "q-89354" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362652 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535710 true] [17592186362652 1100 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of baskets made by a basketball player attempting three free throws if the probability of scoring any free throw is $65%$\n- The number of defective items in a random sample of $100$ items produced by a machine\n- The number of views of a video on a popular video channel" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362652 1113 #uuid "8067fad5-eb5e-5c22-891f-93bc6eeb8202" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362652 1145 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of baskets in $3$ throws can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $3$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of defective items in a random sample of $100$ items can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $100$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of views cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362653 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362654 13194139535710 true] [17592186362653 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362655 13194139535710 true] [17592186362653 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362656 13194139535710 true] [17592186362653 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362657 13194139535710 true] [17592186362653 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362658 13194139535710 true] [17592186362653 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535710 true] [17592186362653 1041 "selection" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362653 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362658 13194139535710 true] [17592186362653 1110 #uuid "b08a43f6-fdc6-5a1f-a83f-7fb7d47352c9" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362654 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362654 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362654 1111 #uuid "58cec676-a0c3-5f27-bd87-fb55dfe6c983" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362655 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362655 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362655 1111 #uuid "63f1d21e-7265-54b1-846a-ed1a4b870c9f" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362656 1034 "I only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362656 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362656 1111 #uuid "c320b944-bc67-5c83-a755-a310061b83ec" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362657 1034 "III only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362657 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362657 1111 #uuid "b42a8f8a-63db-5295-9d03-1fa75bd64a5b" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362658 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362658 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362658 1111 #uuid "e0103aff-aa8c-5935-8572-a9482961e2e4" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362659 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362660 13194139535710 true] [17592186362659 1089 "q-89355" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362659 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535710 true] [17592186362659 1100 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of boys in a family of $3$ children\n- The number of red cars passing through an avenue in an hour\n- The number of pizzas sold by a pizzeria in a day" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362659 1113 #uuid "fc7365bd-65ed-5179-96bc-05b5de5d8f48" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362659 1145 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of boys can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $3$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although being red can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of pizzas sold cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362660 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362661 13194139535710 true] [17592186362660 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362662 13194139535710 true] [17592186362660 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362663 13194139535710 true] [17592186362660 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362664 13194139535710 true] [17592186362660 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362665 13194139535710 true] [17592186362660 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535710 true] [17592186362660 1041 "selection" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362660 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362663 13194139535710 true] [17592186362660 1110 #uuid "1ad8cee0-06d0-550b-968c-ebe823e1b5ac" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362661 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362661 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362661 1111 #uuid "a11b61c8-bfbf-52e4-ab2e-6aa9ece0dce3" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362662 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362662 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362662 1111 #uuid "9129e91a-9091-522f-80d8-de3794b1435b" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362663 1034 "I only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362663 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362663 1111 #uuid "5bae84a9-7824-5533-b448-7bb2a373cca9" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362664 1034 "III only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362664 1111 #uuid "cd0e89a9-d5f4-53f1-b7b6-d01695f81a59" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362665 1034 "II only" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362665 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535710 true] [17592186362665 1111 #uuid "a3f610a3-bd6e-5695-868f-3c0b583dc676" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362666 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362667 13194139535710 true] [17592186362666 1089 "q-89366" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362666 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535710 true] [17592186362666 1100 "A spinner with three regions is designed so that the probability it lands on red is $\\frac{3}{10},$ the probability it lands on blue is $\\frac{2}{5},$ and on green $\\frac{3}{10}.$ What is the probability that the spinner lands on red or green exactly $5$ times if it is spun $12$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362666 1113 #uuid "ed2637f8-1525-514c-9c1e-97be8eb8d1c0" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362666 1145 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the spinner lands on red or green.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ spins\n\n- the probability of landing on red or green on any spin is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,\\frac{3}{5}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=5$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{5}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})}^{12-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{5!(12-5)!}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{7} \\\\  & =792{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{7} \\\\  & =0.101\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535710 true] [17592186362667 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362668 13194139535710 true] [17592186362667 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362669 13194139535710 true] [17592186362667 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362670 13194139535710 true] [17592186362667 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362671 13194139535710 true] [17592186362667 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362672 13194139535710 true] [17592186362667 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535710 true] [17592186362667 1041 "selection" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362667 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362669 13194139535710 true] [17592186362667 1110 #uuid "21db5908-14a7-57fd-9656-f7706596e1ad" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362668 1034 "0.136" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362668 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362668 1111 #uuid "57dfb6e7-4820-5386-aca0-6d10105db075" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362669 1034 "0.101" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362669 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362669 1111 #uuid "5f9155f9-c8dc-5039-a061-b9d44b58b0fd" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362670 1034 "0.089" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362670 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362670 1111 #uuid "5dbeff02-7e8c-5746-abfb-4828d504e26e" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362671 1034 "0.158" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362671 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362671 1111 #uuid "130e6a0f-26d9-561c-822d-7174a582fbe8" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362672 1034 "0.115" 13194139535710 true] [17592186362672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535710 true] [17592186362672 1111 #uuid "213ce3c4-0fd4-5e42-9487-ec1a9d9c3f84" 13194139535710 true]] :edb/tempids {"